From 763a1b18ff14a6e801a339644e69b0531cda88d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 21:15:35 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11122/11171] [outer-loop] Surface the conductor's
 OPERATOR-ATTENTION rows on the dashboard

The run sentinel is report-only by design: 'a false stop is worse than a slow
human'. So its findings need a person, and they were written only to
ops/conductor-log.md, which the hourly check does not read.

At 00:04Z it named an orphaned llama-server holding 21.9 GB across both GPUs
on the conductor's own port. At 00:15Z the same orphan was found and killed by
hand, re-derived from scratch, while the escalation naming it sat unread. The
guard had fired 12 times before and was acted on once, by accident. A
detection nobody reads is a detection that did not happen.

Every kind is named with its count and none is truncated. That clause is
load-bearing rather than tidy: the orphan was 1 row of 18 and the other 17
were one routine kind, so a top-kind-only line would have hidden precisely the
row worth seeing. A mutation proves it.

Rejected: making the sentinel kill the orphan. Its docstring argues the
opposite and that argument is sound; the gap was in reading, not in reaping.

6 tests, 3/3 mutations RED with byte-identical restores. Verified on the live
log: attention AUDIT_FINDING_UNTRIAGED=17 ORPHANED_LLAMA_SERVER=1.

